1
00:00:01,080 --> 00:00:03,960
Start at the origin. Follow the two cyan arrows.

2
00:00:04,940 --> 00:00:10,000
One points a unit to the right. The other points a unit up.

3
00:00:10,780 --> 00:00:14,660
What if four numbers could reshape this entire plane?

4
00:00:15,780 --> 00:00:21,000
Watch the arrowheads move as the grid tilts around them.

5
00:00:23,220 --> 00:00:28,000
The squares now look like thin parallelograms.

6
00:00:28,000 --> 00:00:34,000
Here’s the puzzle. How can their shape change while their area stays the same?

7
00:00:35,410 --> 00:00:40,000
A linear transformation is a map between vector spaces that preserves two operations.

8
00:00:40,000 --> 00:00:44,030
First, addition. Slide the second arrow to the tip of the first.

9
00:00:44,300 --> 00:00:48,140
The diagonal from the origin to the last arrowhead is their sum.

10
00:00:49,970 --> 00:00:53,700
Now transform the whole path. Watch where the diagonal lands.

11
00:00:55,040 --> 00:01:02,000
Transform each short arrow and add them head to tail. You reach exactly the same point.

12
00:01:02,880 --> 00:01:07,910
The second rule is scaling. Here we double the length of an arrow.

13
00:01:07,910 --> 00:01:14,600
Transform that double arrow, and you get twice the transformed arrow. The rule works for any scalar.

14
00:01:15,300 --> 00:01:20,950
This is the matrix behind our animation. Let’s call it A.

15
00:01:20,950 --> 00:01:25,850
We write the input as a column: horizontal steps on top, vertical steps below.

16
00:01:27,880 --> 00:01:33,870
Start with the first basis vector. One step right, zero steps up.

17
00:01:33,870 --> 00:01:40,000
One selects the first column of A. Zero cancels the second.

18
00:01:41,240 --> 00:01:45,080
Follow the tip. It moves two units right and one unit up.

19
00:01:46,040 --> 00:01:51,240
Those coordinates are exactly the entries of the first column.

20
00:01:51,950 --> 00:01:56,350
Now start with the second basis vector: zero steps right, one step up.

21
00:01:57,410 --> 00:02:04,000
This selects the second column. The tip lands one unit right and one unit up.

22
00:02:06,170 --> 00:02:11,000
What about an arbitrary vector? Think of its coordinates as counts of basis steps.

23
00:02:11,000 --> 00:02:18,000
Keep the counts. Replace the two original steps with their images, and add them together.

24
00:02:20,410 --> 00:02:25,930
Let’s use those two steps to follow an entire unit square.

25
00:02:25,930 --> 00:02:30,640
Mark the origin, both basis tips, and the far corner they reach together.

26
00:02:32,970 --> 00:02:38,530
Watch each family of parallel lines tilt together as the grid changes shape.

27
00:02:38,850 --> 00:02:45,000
The origin stays fixed. Zero steps in either direction still gives zero.

28
00:02:45,930 --> 00:02:51,000
The far corner began at one, one. Add the two transformed basis vectors.

29
00:02:51,000 --> 00:02:56,830
Two plus one gives three. One plus one gives two. That’s the far corner of our parallelogram.

30
00:02:59,000 --> 00:03:03,850
Now try the vector one, two: one horizontal step and two vertical steps.

31
00:03:04,680 --> 00:03:09,190
Under A, that means one copy of the first column and two copies of the second.

32
00:03:11,050 --> 00:03:15,000
Trace those three steps head to tail. Where do they end?

33
00:03:15,000 --> 00:03:19,530
Four units right and three units up. That’s the orange arrow.

34
00:03:20,740 --> 00:03:25,090
That parallelogram looks smaller than the square. Does it actually cover less area?

35
00:03:25,870 --> 00:03:33,000
The determinant tells us how area changes, and whether the map reverses orientation.

36
00:03:33,770 --> 00:03:39,020
For this two-by-two matrix, multiply the main diagonal entries: two times one.

37
00:03:40,350 --> 00:03:46,000
Then subtract the product on the other diagonal: one times one.

38
00:03:47,090 --> 00:03:49,920
Two minus one leaves a determinant of one.

39
00:03:51,050 --> 00:03:56,230
Its absolute value is the area scale factor. One square unit stays one square unit.

40
00:03:56,370 --> 00:03:59,850
The edges still change length and direction.

41
00:04:00,940 --> 00:04:07,010
Now trace the corners in order. A positive determinant keeps a counterclockwise trip counterclockwise.

42
00:04:09,010 --> 00:04:13,780
Reflect the square across the vertical axis. The same trip now runs clockwise.

43
00:04:14,140 --> 00:04:16,740
This reflection has determinant negative one.

44
00:04:17,990 --> 00:04:22,700
Finally, project the square onto the horizontal axis. Watch the upper corners drop.

45
00:04:23,150 --> 00:04:28,330
The area vanishes. A zero determinant collapses the plane to a line or even a single point.

46
00:04:30,400 --> 00:04:35,010
Follow one arrow, and you see an input vector become an output vector.

47
00:04:35,210 --> 00:04:38,440
Follow both basis arrows, and you can predict the whole grid.

48
00:04:39,020 --> 00:04:43,000
The determinant gives the area scale through its magnitude, and orientation through its sign.

49
00:04:43,300 --> 00:04:47,960
A matrix does more than multiply numbers. It transforms geometry,

50
00:04:48,370 --> 00:04:53,200
changes directions, reshapes space, and reveals the hidden structure of linear algebra.

51
00:04:54,830 --> 00:04:58,000
Thank you for watching.
